>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

When the timer_sleep() is called, it checks if elapsed time is smaller
than the ticks. If it is true, it calls thread_sleep() to put the current
thread to sleep until current tick becomes "start_tick + ticks".
Also, the role that wakes up the thread is distributed to the timer_interrupt().

>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?

We stored the earlist wake up ticks of the threads in the sleep_list
at the global variable next_ticks_to_awake. Then, timer_interrupt()
needs to call thread_awake() only after the current tick is not less
than the next_ticks_to_awake. Therefore, timer_interrupt() does not
need to check all threads in the sleep_list to wake each tick.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?

In PintOS, there's only one thread is running, and also, thread_sleep()
put current thread to sleep only. Therefore, even though multiple threads
call timer_sleep(), only one thread(the running thread) put to sleep.

>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?

The race conditions are avoided by disabling the interrupt until
every thing in the thread_sleep() are done.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A6: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

Before we changed the design, the CPU needs to check every time to wake
the threads up, which is too inefficient because sleeping threads occupy
the CPU. However after we changed the design, the CPU only needs to wake
up the threads when it's time to wake up, and the sleeping threads do not
occupy the CPU anymore. Since, this design only does the least amount of
work, we choose this design.

